<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malcolm X</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1925">1925</a>–<a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Malcolm Little, el-Hajj Malik el-Shabazz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malcolm X was an African American Muslim minister and civil rights leader. Born Malcolm Little, he joined the Nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in prison and from 1952 was its public face, urging Black empowerment and separation from white Americans and criticising the mainstream civil rights movement's emphasis on non-violence and integration. He left the Nation in 1964, made the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> and founded the Organization of Afro-American Unity. He was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in February 1965; his ideas were an important influence on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-power-movement">Black power movement</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malcolm-x.jpg" alt="Malcolm X" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marion S. Trikosko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malcolm_X_in_1964.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Malcolm X was born on 19 May 1925 in Omaha, Nebraska, the fourth of seven children of Louise Little, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a>, and the Reverend Earl Little, a Baptist minister. Both parents admired the Pan-African activist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-garvey">Marcus Garvey</a> and led the local branch of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-negro-improvement-association">Universal Negro Improvement Association</a>, and they taught their children self-reliance and black pride. In 1946 he was sentenced to eight to ten years in prison for larceny and burglary. There he met a fellow convict, John Bembry, who gave him a voracious appetite for reading, and joined the Nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, taking the name Malcolm X in place of what he called the white slavemaster name of Little.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After his parole in August 1952 Malcolm X rose quickly in the Nation. He took charge of Temple Number 7 in Harlem in 1954 and became its public face for twelve years, preaching black empowerment and the separation of Black and white Americans, and criticising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a> and the mainstream civil rights movement for its emphasis on non-violence and integration. The Federal Bureau of Investigation began watching him in 1953. He is credited with helping the Nation grow from about 1,200 members in the early 1950s to an estimated 50,000 to 100,000 by the early 1960s.</p><p>On 8 March 1964 he announced that he was leaving the Nation. He founded Muslim Mosque, Inc., a religious organisation, and the Organization of Afro-American Unity, a secular group advocating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism</a>. In April 1964 he made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, and he then toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Throughout 1964 Malcolm X received death threats as his conflict with the Nation intensified. On 21 February 1965 he was shot as he prepared to address the Organization of Afro-American Unity at the Audubon Ballroom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a>. Three Nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> members were convicted, and in 2021 two of the convictions were vacated. Whether others conceived or aided the killing has been debated since.</p><p>Apart from his autobiography, written with Alex Haley and published posthumously in 1965, Malcolm X left no published writings, and his philosophy is known from his speeches and interviews. His ideas were an important influence on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-power-movement">Black power movement</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malcolm X” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Malcolm X</b>, the best-known minister of the Nation of Islam until 1964, rejected integration into a white society and urged Black pride and self-reliance, and from 1966 the call for “Black Power” asked for Black-led organisations, political power and community control.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>In the diaspora, Pan-African ideas fed the civil rights and Black Power movements in the United States, where <b>Malcolm X</b> founded the Organization of Afro-American Unity in 1964 on the model of the OAU, and the independence and cultural movements of the Caribbean, among them Rastafari, which arose…</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Malcolm X</b> was shot dead as he began speaking at the Audubon Ballroom in New York.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-21">21 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Omaha, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.26,-95.94&amp;year=1951" aria-label="Omaha on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2207 692 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Malcolm X"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2657 962h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2657 962h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.26,-95.94&amp;year=1951">What was here in 1951? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
